    <article class="prose">
      <section id="problem" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">01 / The problem</p>
        <h2>Recognising action across space and time.</h2>
        <p class="lead">A still image can show appearance. An action is defined by how that appearance changes from one frame to the next.</p>
        <p>This group project studied two connected video-understanding tasks. The first assigns one of 25 action labels to a short clip. The second predicts the actor’s class and location across the clip, producing a continuous action tube rather than a single image-level decision.</p>
        <p>Three pretrained transformer architectures—TimeSFormer, VideoMAE and ViViT—were fine-tuned and compared. The most practical backbone was then extended with a DETR-style detection head for spatio-temporal localisation.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="classification" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">02 / Classification</p>
        <h2>Choosing the strongest practical backbone.</h2>
        <p>VideoMAE produced the best validation top-1 accuracy and weighted F1 while taking roughly half the training time of ViViT. This made it the most useful accuracy–efficiency compromise for the wider experimental programme.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Architecture</th><th>Val top-1</th><th>Val top-5</th><th>Weighted F1</th><th>Runtime</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>TimeSFormer</td><td>83.96%</td><td>94.65%</td><td>83.28%</td><td>18m 42s</td></tr>
              <tr><td>VideoMAE</td><td>88.77%</td><td>97.86%</td><td>88.53%</td><td>19m 14s</td></tr>
              <tr><td>ViViT</td><td>87.70%</td><td>99.47%</td><td>87.40%</td><td>38m 06s</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Ablations explored frame count, temporal sampling, fine-tuning depth, data augmentation and learning-rate schedules. Accuracy improved as temporal coverage increased to 24 frames, while a 32-frame input added cost without improving the result. Full fine-tuning and label-preserving augmentations were especially important.</p>
        <div class="stat-grid">
          <div class="stat-card"><strong>91.49%</strong><span>held-out HMDB top-1 accuracy</span></div>
          <div class="stat-card"><strong>99.47%</strong><span>held-out top-5 accuracy</span></div>
          <div class="stat-card"><strong>91.33%</strong><span>macro F1 across 25 classes</span></div>
          <div class="stat-card"><strong>24</strong><span>frames in the final classifier input</span></div>
        </div>
        <figure>
          <img src="/images/action-recognition/training-dynamics.png" alt="VideoMAE training curves showing loss, top-1 accuracy and top-5 accuracy" width="1800" height="612" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Figure 1. Training dynamics for the final VideoMAE classifier. Source: project report.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="localisation" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">03 / Localisation</p>
        <h2>From a clip label to an action tube.</h2>
        <p>The VideoMAE backbone was connected to a transformer decoder with learned action queries. Separate heads predicted class, a normalised tubelet box and its temporal extent. Hungarian matching paired predictions with ground truth during training.</p>
        <figure>
          <img src="/images/action-recognition/architecture.png" alt="Architecture of the VideoMAE and DETR-style spatio-temporal action-localisation system" width="1774" height="887" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Figure 2. The final VideoMAE–DETR localisation pipeline. Source: project report.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Four decoder layers and five object queries gave the best balance. Multi-layer feature fusion helped because localisation needs spatial detail that may be weakened in the most semantic final backbone layer. The strongest aggressively fine-tuned tubelet configuration reached 78.57% frame-mAP and 84.91% video-mAP.</p>
        <figure>
          <img src="/images/action-recognition/localisation-strip-a.png" alt="Video strips comparing ground-truth and predicted boxes for accurate action localisations" width="2400" height="774" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Figure 3. Example action tubes across video frames. Source: project report.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="analysis" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">04 / Understanding errors</p>
        <h2>Attention can be plausible and still be wrong.</h2>
        <p>Most classification mistakes occurred between visually adjacent movements: throwing and picking, drawing a sword and shooting a bow, or waving and smoking. The correct class was usually still among the model’s top five choices, suggesting fine-grained semantic ambiguity rather than a complete failure to understand the clip.</p>
        <p>Localisation failures were mainly categorical or spatial. Smaller actors were more difficult to localise accurately, while temporal-only failure was not the main bottleneck on JHMDB.</p>
        <figure>
          <img src="/images/action-recognition/localisation-summary.png" alt="Best and worst JHMDB action classes measured by frame mean average precision" width="1500" height="675" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Figure 4. Per-class variation in localisation performance. Source: project report.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="outcome" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">05 / Outcome</p>
        <h2>Good video features transfer—but localisation remains its own problem.</h2>
        <p class="lead">The project connected systematic backbone selection with an end-to-end detection system and analysis of where each stage succeeds or fails.</p>
        <p>VideoMAE offered the strongest practical foundation, but classification strength alone did not guarantee spatial precision. Fine-tuning, multi-level features and a temporally coherent tubelet representation were critical once the task moved from recognising an action to locating it.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
